{
  "cases": [
    {
      "id": "P1A",
      "instrument_review": "EFPA §§9.3, 9.7 and 9.8: reinterpret test-retest precision as consistency of a fixed AI across repeated evaluations. One score without execution history leaves precision unrateable (0), rather than demonstrating instability.",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "claim1",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "gives no execution history",
          "reason": "The passage does not establish whether repeated evaluations occurred."
        },
        {
          "id": "claim2",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "one headline score of 75%",
          "reason": "A single reported score cannot demonstrate variation across identical evaluations."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "P1B",
      "instrument_review": "EFPA §§9.3, 9.7 and 9.8: the execution record explicitly establishes that repeatability was not studied. Precision remains unrateable (0); absence of repetitions does not demonstrate unstable scores.",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "claim1",
          "label": "Contradicted",
          "quote": "no repeat evaluations were conducted",
          "reason": "The record explicitly rules out measuring changes across repeated evaluations."
        },
        {
          "id": "claim2",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "this was the only evaluation performed",
          "reason": "Without repetitions, the findings establish neither stability nor instability."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "P2A",
      "instrument_review": "EFPA §§9.3 and 9.7: six identical-condition evaluations provide repeatability evidence. The two-percentage-point range meets the report's three-point rule. This supports stability for this comparison, without establishing broader precision or importing EFPA human-sample thresholds.",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "claim1",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "identical inputs, settings and resources on six occasions",
          "reason": "The same AI and problems were evaluated repeatedly under matching conditions."
        },
        {
          "id": "claim2",
          "label": "Contradicted",
          "quote": "73, 74, 74, 75, 74 and 73 percent",
          "reason": "The range is two percentage points, within the stated maximum of three."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "P2B",
      "instrument_review": "EFPA §§9.3 and 9.7: repeated identical-condition evaluations directly examine precision. The 31-percentage-point range fails the report's three-point stability requirement, providing evidence of inadequate stability for this comparison.",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "claim1",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "identical inputs, settings and resources on six occasions",
          "reason": "The passage explicitly describes repeated evaluations under the same conditions."
        },
        {
          "id": "claim2",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "51, 66, 79, 55, 82 and 62 percent",
          "reason": "The range is 31 percentage points, exceeding the stated maximum of three."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "P3A",
      "instrument_review": "EFPA §§6.2, 6.3.2, 9 and 10.1 distinguish documentation from technical evidence. Topic and scoring descriptions do not establish repeatability or answer correctness; the relevant findings are insufficient to rate (0).",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "claim1",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "It gives no execution history",
          "reason": "No evidence establishes that identical-condition repetitions were studied."
        },
        {
          "id": "claim2",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "no findings about mistakes in the provided answers",
          "reason": "Missing examination findings do not demonstrate answer errors."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "P3B",
      "instrument_review": "EFPA §§6.2 and 6.3.8: the appendix adds operational documentation, not reliability or content-quality findings. Under §§9 and 10.1, repeatability and answer correctness remain unestablished.",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "claim1",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "It reports no further evaluation results",
          "reason": "Installation information adds no evidence of repeated identical-condition evaluations."
        },
        {
          "id": "claim2",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "no further evaluation results or examination of answers",
          "reason": "The appendix supplies no findings demonstrating answer mistakes."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "P4A",
      "instrument_review": "EFPA §§6.1.3.2, 6.1.3.3 and 10.1: independent examination supports a development review process, not universal answer correctness. Reconciliation without initial agreement data cannot establish reviewer agreement; §9.6 applies only if such judgements contribute to operational scoring.",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "claim1",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "Two editors independently examined every question and supplied answer before release.",
          "reason": "Both item questions and answer keys underwent development examination."
        },
        {
          "id": "claim2",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "does not state whether mistakes were found or how many remained",
          "reason": "The review process does not establish that every released answer is correct."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "P4B",
      "instrument_review": "EFPA §§6.1.3.2, 6.1.7 and 10.1: development review occurred, but twelve released answer errors provide direct adverse evidence about item and scoring-key quality. Missing initial agreement data remain an evidence gap, separate from the established errors.",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "claim1",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "Two editors independently examined every question and supplied answer before release.",
          "reason": "The passage explicitly establishes development examination of both."
        },
        {
          "id": "claim2",
          "label": "Contradicted",
          "quote": "twelve supplied answers as wrong in the released version",
          "reason": "Identified released answer errors explicitly refute universal correctness."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "P5A",
      "instrument_review": "EFPA §§3.1–3.2 and 6.3.2: deterministic marking supports repeatability for identical text, not correctness. Correctness checks are unreported and unrateable (0). Under §9.6, inter-rater reliability is inapplicable to the described fixed rule without judgemental scoring.",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "claim1",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "No comparison of those marks with independently established correct marks is reported.",
          "reason": "Unreported testing does not establish either that testing occurred or that none occurred."
        },
        {
          "id": "claim2",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "identical text always receives the same mark",
          "reason": "Consistent marks can still be incorrect; universal scoring accuracy is unsupported."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "P5B",
      "instrument_review": "EFPA §6.3.2: comparison against independent correct marks tests scoring implementation and reveals unresolved errors. Determinism does not resolve those errors or establish validity. The 20 chosen responses do not establish coverage of all possible responses.",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "claim1",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "tested the software against independently established correct marks for 20 chosen responses",
          "reason": "The described comparison directly establishes that correctness testing occurred."
        },
        {
          "id": "claim2",
          "label": "Contradicted",
          "quote": "Two marks differed and the discrepancies remain unresolved.",
          "reason": "Reported discrepancies with independently correct marks refute correctness for every response."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "P6A",
      "instrument_review": "EFPA §§2.2, 3.2 and 10.5: validity concerns the stated interpretation. The counts support the narrowly descriptive comparison. Under §8.3.2.2, norms are inapplicable to this sole raw-score ranking purpose; broader generalisation is unsupported.",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "claim1",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "identifying which system solved more of these 48 grid problems in this evaluation",
          "reason": "The reported solution counts directly answer this restricted question."
        },
        {
          "id": "claim2",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "A solves 36 and B solves 24.",
          "reason": "A solved more evaluated problems under the reported fixed conditions."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "P6B",
      "instrument_review": "EFPA §§6.1.1, 10.1 and 10.5: the broad general-intelligence interpretation requires evidence beyond this task set and setting. The narrow comparison is supported, but generalisation is not established; missing evidence does not prove the benchmark incapable of measuring the intended construct.",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "claim1",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "No results from other tasks or settings are supplied.",
          "reason": "The findings do not justify a conclusion across subjects and real-world settings."
        },
        {
          "id": "claim2",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "A solves 36 and B solves 24.",
          "reason": "The counts establish A's advantage on these evaluated grid problems."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "P7A",
      "instrument_review": "EFPA §§3.2, 6.1.3.1 and 10.5: scoring should match its intended interpretation. Binary whole-grid credit meets the explicit requirement; omission of partial credit is deliberate and is not evidence of a scoring defect for this purpose.",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "claim1",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "count only completely correct output grids",
          "reason": "One point for a completely correct grid and zero otherwise implements the requirement."
        },
        {
          "id": "claim2",
          "label": "Contradicted",
          "quote": "partial correctness is deliberately outside this comparison",
          "reason": "Zero credit for partial correctness follows the stated requirement."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "P7B",
      "instrument_review": "EFPA §§3.2, 6.1.3.1 and 10.5: the implemented whole-grid rule conflicts with the required cell-level credit. This is direct evidence of inadequate scoring alignment (1), rather than merely missing validation evidence.",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "claim1",
          "label": "Contradicted",
          "quote": "give a response credit for every correctly completed cell",
          "reason": "The binary rule withholds required credit for correct cells in partially correct grids."
        },
        {
          "id": "claim2",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "A partially correct grid receives zero.",
          "reason": "Zero credit conflicts with the requirement to credit correctly completed cells."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "P8A",
      "instrument_review": "EFPA §§2.8, 9.3 and 10.3.1.6: instruction wording is an administration condition. This design examines sensitivity to changed instructions, not identical-condition repeatability. Without identical-instruction repetitions, it does not separately quantify repeatability noise.",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "claim1",
          "label": "Contradicted",
          "quote": "each evaluation uses a differently worded instruction",
          "reason": "An administration condition changes on every evaluation."
        },
        {
          "id": "claim2",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "reports how its scores change",
          "reason": "The study compares scores while varying instruction wording and holding the listed other conditions fixed; it does not isolate the effect from repeatability noise."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "P8B",
      "instrument_review": "EFPA §§2.8, 9.3 and 9.7: the design examines score changes under identical administration conditions. It does not examine instruction sensitivity. Without score values or precision estimates, the magnitude and adequacy of repeatability remain unrateable (0).",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "claim1",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "software, resources, problem order, settings and instruction are identical on every evaluation",
          "reason": "The reported score-change study holds the specified conditions constant."
        },
        {
          "id": "claim2",
          "label": "Contradicted",
          "quote": "No different instruction or presentation is studied.",
          "reason": "The study explicitly excludes instruction variation."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "closing_note": "No task instruction required clarification. The supplied EFPA Test Review Model v5.0 (EFPA, 2025; https://www.efpa.eu/; CC BY-SA 4.0) was retained, with selective case explanations as permitted. Across Parts A–C, test taker was reinterpreted as the evaluated AI, administration as its evaluation conditions, and intended use as the claimed score interpretation. Human identity, consent, sensory or motor accessibility, clinical feedback and professional qualification questions are inapplicable to these AI test takers; human users' documentation needs remain relevant. Sections 6–12 were considered: unreported development, materials, norms, precision, validity, fair-use and report evidence remains unestablished, not automatically inadequate or inapplicable. AI-group comparisons require explicit reinterpretation; no human-group fairness findings are implied. Digital result cards do not establish availability of EFPA-style interpretive reports, and the required three report profiles were not supplied. Ratings mentioned retain EFPA meanings: 0 insufficient information, 1 inadequate, 2 adequate, 3 good, 4 excellent, n/a inapplicable. Claim labels are separate. EFPA's restriction to research or exceptional expert use applies where a critical attribute receives 0 or 1; no unrestricted broader use is justified here. No aggregate quality score or EFPA endorsement is implied. Only supplied passages and the supplied model were used; bibliography references were not independently consulted."
}
